  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA disturbance noted in water vapor imagery north of the eastern \nGreat Lakes early this afternoon, will move across northern NY \nlater this afternoon into this evening. This disturbance will \nbring isolated to scattered showers to areas well north of I-90.\nDue to a cold core aloft still in place and fairly steep mid and\nlow level lapse rates, some small hail and wind gusts up to 40 \nmph may occur in some low-topped convective showers in the W. \nAdirondacks. It will remain breezy this afternoon due to \ncontinued good mixing conditions, with west-northwest winds \ngusting 20-30 mph at times and especially in favored areas for \nchanneling such as the Mohawk Valley, Capital District, N. \nTaconics and Berkshires. Temperatures look to be near normal \nthis afternoon. High pressure then builds in tonight through \nSat, providing dry and mainly clear conditions along with \nlighter winds and seasonably cool temperatures. Patchy frost is \npossible in the upper Hudson Valley tonight and Sat night. If \nconfidence in more widespread frost increases, Frost Advisories \nmay be needed.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next likelihood of widespread rain arrives Sunday afternoon,\nas the remnant moisture from post-T.C. Isaias moves into the \nregion from the south/west. Guidance in good agreement tracking \nthe weakened surface cyclone from the mid Atlantic region to \njust south of NYC Sun night into early Mon. While deep layer \nmoisture will be above normal (PWAT anomalies of +2 to +3 STDEV \nfrom the NAEFS), the best forcing should be displaced to the \nsouth along with a weakening upper level trough by the time it \nreaches our area. There is high confidence in widespread \nrainfall, with total amounts of mainly 0.5-1.0\". Latest NBM \nprobabilities for > 1.0\" of rain are mainly 35%-55% with the \nhighest probs south/west of Albany. Probs for > 2.0\" are < 10%. \nGiven several days of dry weather preceding this rainfall along \nwith high confidence in overall rainfall amounts of < 2.0\", \nflooding is not anticipated. The rain is expected to end early \nMon morning, as the progressive system quickly tracks east off \nof the coast and surface ridging builds in Mon afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cold front along with a potential wave of low pressure, is \nexpected to move across the region next Tue into Tue night. \nThere is still quite a bit of spread in the deterministic and \nensemble guidance with regards to the strength/track of the \nwave, which will have an impact on how widespread showers will \nbe along with duration of precip and wind speeds. For now will \ncontinue to mention chance PoPs across much of the area Tue into\nTue night, with likely values across the W. Adirondacks and S. \nVT. Behind the front, a cooler air mass will filter in as a \nlarge area of high pressure builds east across the Great Lakes \nand Northeast. However, noting temperatures do not look to be \nquite as chilly as it appeared yesterday. Still, highs could \nstill be close to 5 degrees below normal for mid October Wed-\nThu. It also should be dry during this time.",
